<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  W3C SCXML 3.4: a region that took an external self-transition still holds an
  atomic state, so it answers the NEXT event too.

  Its sibling `parallel_regions_take_own_transitions` owns the microstep axis —
  did every region enabled by one event take its own transition, and run its
  content. This fixture owns the axis after that one: a region can satisfy the
  sibling's question in full and still have been left holding no leaf, and the
  difference between the two is one more event.

  ## Why one more event is a different question

  A `<parallel>` region that keeps its root and loses its leaf is present in
  the configuration by every structural test — `run` is active, `budget` is
  active — while nothing inside it can ever fire again. There is no error, no
  warning, and no failed assertion at the moment it happens. It is found later,
  by an event that the region was supposed to answer and did not.

  Measured 2026-08-14 against the defect
  `sce-build/tests/mutations/parallel_microstep_owns_exit_and_entry.cases`
  restores: with the defect in place, the sibling fixture's whole driver stays
  green (SURVIVED, 0/2 red). The two fixtures differ in exactly one thing that
  matters here — which region the engine's `currentState_` is resting in when
  the event arrives.

  ## Why the self-transitioning region comes FIRST

  The double application is `exit(oldState)` then `enter(currentState_)`, where
  `oldState` is where the engine's single current-state pointer was before the
  event. When `oldState` belongs to a region the microstep has already moved
  OUT of, re-exiting it removes a state that is no longer active and nothing
  breaks. The damage needs `oldState` to be a leaf the microstep RE-ENTERED,
  which is what an external self-transition does — and that requires the
  pointer to be resting in the self-transitioning region.

  So the ordering below is load-bearing rather than stylistic: `budget`, the
  shallower region whose transition is an external self-transition, is first in
  document order, and `drive` is second. Reversing them is the sibling
  fixture's shape, and the sibling fixture cannot see this defect.

  ## The verdict

  `drive` answers `e` once and then goes quiet — `judging` has no `e`
  transition — so the SECOND `e` is a question only `budget` can answer. Its
  `<assign>` is the answer: `m` reaches 2 only if the region still had a leaf
  to transition from. The top-level `<final id="settled">` is guarded on
  `n == 1 && m == 2`, so a region that moved without running its content fails
  the same way one that could not move at all does, and a top-level final is
  the one observable every backend exposes — including the ones that report a
  single current leaf rather than a configuration.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml" version="1.0"
       name="parallel_self_transition_keeps_its_leaf" datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="run">

  <datamodel>
    <data id="n" expr="0"/>
    <data id="m" expr="0"/>
  </datamodel>

  <parallel id="run">

    <!--
      The shallower region, FIRST in document order. Its transition on `e` is
      an external self-transition: Appendix D resolves the domain through
      `findLCCA` over the proper ancestors, so the exit set is `{within}` and
      the microstep exits and re-enters it. That re-entry is what a second,
      caller-supplied exit then undoes.
    -->
    <state id="budget" initial="within">
      <state id="within">
        <transition event="e" target="within">
          <assign location="m" expr="m + 1"/>
        </transition>
      </state>
    </state>

    <!--
      The deeper region, SECOND. It answers the first `e` and then has nothing
      to say, which is what leaves the second `e` addressed to `budget` alone.
    -->
    <state id="drive" initial="running">
      <state id="running" initial="working">
        <state id="working">
          <transition event="e" target="judging">
            <assign location="n" expr="n + 1"/>
          </transition>
        </state>
        <state id="judging">
          <!--
            The document decides its own verdict rather than leaving it to the
            host: `m == 2` is reachable only if `budget` was still holding
            `within` when the second `e` arrived.
          -->
          <transition event="check" cond="n == 1 &amp;&amp; m == 2" target="settled"/>
        </state>
      </state>
    </state>

  </parallel>

  <final id="settled"/>
</scxml>
